WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.840
Throughout the conflict in Ukraine,&nbsp;
significant territorial shifts have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.440
occurred. Russia, at one point, advanced close to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:11.240
major urban centers such as Kyiv and Kherson,&nbsp;
occupying farmlands and strategic rural areas.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.320
While their military presence has&nbsp;
since diminished, they have left a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.160
lasting and destructive legacy&nbsp;
on Ukrainian soil: landmines.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.040
A cruel and long-lasting weapon designed to act as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:28.040
a deterrent for military personnel moving in&nbsp;
to recapture land taken from them. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:32.360
once the positions are abandoned the&nbsp;
mines stay. Concealed underground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:38.080
they continue to pose significant threats for&nbsp;
years or decades after the conflict's conclusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:44.240
hindering agricultural activities, restricting&nbsp;
civilian mobility, and causing lasting trauma.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.000
Starting in 2014 with the&nbsp;
Russian occupation of Crimea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.840
the southern part of Ukraine became&nbsp;
contaminated with minefields. [1][REF]

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:57.880
7 years of tedious work went into demining&nbsp;
an area of 23,000 square kilometers.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.040
In 18 months, all that work went to waste,

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:07.400
as this area plus another 150,000 square&nbsp;
kilometers, is now contaminated with mines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:12.640
once again. This accounts for 30% of&nbsp;
Ukraine’s total land area, roughly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:18.880
size of Florida. Making Ukraine the country&nbsp;
with the largest area contaminated by mines.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.160
The regions affected are concentrated in the east,&nbsp;
where most of the conflict is happening but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.800
include areas near the country's capital, Kiev,&nbsp;
where the Russian army got worryingly close to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:35.960
2022. These mines are already starting to take&nbsp;
a toll on the Ukrainian people as 298 civilians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:41.560
have been killed, 22 of them being children,&nbsp;
with over 600 injured. [2]REF] Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.240
these numbers are only going to grow. It&nbsp;
is estimated that it will take Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:52.480
750 years to clear its land using current&nbsp;
demining and detection techniques.[3][REF]

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:57.200
So what exactly are landmines, and&nbsp;
what tools do we have to detect and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:06.960
destroy them? Let's delve deeper into&nbsp;
the painful task facing Ukraine. 
 
 

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.000
There are two types of landmines, larger ones that&nbsp;
are designed to disable or destroy large vehicles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.960
like tanks and smaller ones&nbsp;
designed to maim individuals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:20.440
and Ukraine is unfortunately littered with both.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:27.240
The Russian PFM-1 [4][REF] is a small plastic case&nbsp;
mine filled with 37 grams of liquid explosives.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.840
Nicknamed the butterfly mine, their small&nbsp;
wings allow them to be dropped from the air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:37.280
whether by mortar, helicopter or airplane.
The unsymmetrical wings allow them to glide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.880
and spin to the ground like a helicopter&nbsp;
seed. Landing softly on the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:46.560
waiting for an unsuspecting victim.
When stood on, the thin plastic deforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.080
which increases the pressure on the liquid&nbsp;
inside the casing, causing it to explode.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.960
Because they are camouflaged&nbsp;
and contain little metal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:58.160
detection of these sinister weapons is&nbsp;
extremely challenging. Their lightweight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.400
nature also allows them to be carried&nbsp;
by water, potentially reaching civilian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.560
areas where children can mistake them for&nbsp;
toys, making them particularly inhumane.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.000
The Russian PMN-2 Landmine&nbsp;
is a general-purpose mine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.120
that also uses a plastic casing&nbsp;
to minimize metal components.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.760
It is buried between 5 to 10&nbsp;
centimeters below the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.440
and contains around 100g of TNT&nbsp;
[5][REF][REF-old]. It detonates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:27.520
with a force of around 15 kilograms, low&nbsp;
enough to be triggered by stepping on it.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.680
But, mines aren’t only activated by pressure.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.360
To create impediments along roads&nbsp;
or entrances to building the OZM-72&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:41.720
Landmine uses a trip wire as a trigger&nbsp;
to explode 660 grams of TNT. [6][REF]

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.840
Anti-tank mines, unlike those targeting&nbsp;
individuals, require more TNT to penetrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:52.600
heavily armored tanks and are designed to detonate&nbsp;
under larger pressures. The M1 Abrams tanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:58.960
recently sent to Ukraine will face TM-62 land&nbsp;
mines, which detonate only when objects weighing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:06.240
over 150 kilograms pass over them, delivering a&nbsp;
powerful 7.5 kilograms TNT explosion. [7] [REF]

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.360
Since 2021, the Russian army&nbsp;
has employed the PTKM-1R,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:16.080
a novel mine designed to target the upper&nbsp;
and less armored sections of the tanks.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:19.320
The mine is not triggered&nbsp;
by passing over it, instead

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.840
it is equipped with two seismic sensors&nbsp;
for detecting tank movements and four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.280
microphones to capture loud sounds. [8][REF]

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.480
Once a tank is detected,it tilts&nbsp;
60 degrees toward the target and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.560
launches a munition 30 meters above the tank.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:39.160
In a parabolic trajectory, infrared and radar&nbsp;
sensors activate the second explosive phase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.720
deploying a shaped charge to penetrate&nbsp;
the tank from above. [9] [REF]

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.280
Tools to help detect and destroy these&nbsp;
mines have mostly come from the US.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.760
Anti-mine equipment is listed as part of the&nbsp;
latest 600 million dollar aid package to Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:57.240
in September 2023. [10][REF].&nbsp;
What do these tools look like?

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:02.040
The tools chosen to demine an area depend&nbsp;
on the current phase of the conflict and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:06.960
the lands intended use after the mine&nbsp;
has been removed. Military demining,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:11.360
intended to establish secure pathways&nbsp;
for highly trained soldiers and heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.520
reinforced equipment, is carried out&nbsp;
very differently from demining efforts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.080
in civilian areas used for farming&nbsp;
or where children will play freely.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.680
So how does the military deal with&nbsp;
all these mines? With brute force.

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:26.920
Mine clearance line charges can create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:31.280
secure path in minutes. [11][REF].
These are long lines containing C4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:36.480
explosive pellets, attached to a central&nbsp;
nylon rope along with detonation wires.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.360
The line is attached to a solid propellant&nbsp;
rocket placed on an angled guide. When fired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:47.040
it pulls the 100 meter line taut along the&nbsp;
desired path. Then a line is detonated.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.720
The explosion creates enough pressure&nbsp;
to detonate any mines below the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:55.680
4m at each side of the line. [11]&nbsp;
[REF]. If a longer path is needed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.480
machine will roll along the recently&nbsp;
created path and repeat the process.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:04.240
This method prioritizes speed, for&nbsp;
when enemies are nearby or troops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:09.400
need to move quickly to the front lines,&nbsp;
but this method comes at a high costs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:12.520
each line costs $83,000 thousands. [12][REF]

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:16.960
But what if speed is not as important?&nbsp;
Like when the enemy has retreated and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.800
you need to secure key positions.&nbsp;
In some cases fields have been set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.360
on fire in Ukraine to clear them&nbsp;
of the small plastic PFM-1 mines.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.720
Armored tanks, resistant to smaller&nbsp;
mine explosions outfitted with tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:33.760
to either detonate or push&nbsp;
aside mines can also be used.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:37.760
One method to do this uses chain linked&nbsp;
flails. As the vehicle moves forward these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:42.960
flails strike and detonate the mines. [13][REF].&nbsp;
This often breaks the flail, causing chain links&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:48.480
to fly off into the distance, but additional&nbsp;
links can easily be attached to repair them.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:51.760
Detonation may not always be&nbsp;
necessary to clear a path. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:56.160
plow attachment on the M1 Abrams tank&nbsp;
can dig into the soil beneath the mines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:00.240
and push mines out of the way without&nbsp;
triggering them from above. [14] [REF]

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:05.400
In recent years, smaller remote controlled flailed&nbsp;
vehicles have started to operate. [13][REF]

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:11.000
Ukrainian farmers desperate to start cultivating&nbsp;
again have even retrofitted some of their farming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:16.160
equipment to be remote-controlled and have started&nbsp;
to unofficially demine their lands. [15] [REF]

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:21.120
Using armored vehicles like these for mine&nbsp;
clearing does have some limitations. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:27.240
unsuitable for rough terrain and dense vegetation,&nbsp;
where maneuverability is compromised. In open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:33.560
areas, such as those in Ukraine, they become&nbsp;
conspicuous targets that the enemy can anticipate.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:37.720
Ukrainian soldiers have reported that the&nbsp;
Leopard tank is particularly vulnerable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:42.840
when engaged in minefield clearance. It is&nbsp;
frequently targeted by the Russian Army since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:47.600
disabling the tank will significantly&nbsp;
impede the Ukrainian forces' progress.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:51.120
These methods can remove 80%&nbsp;
to 90% of mines. [16] [REF]

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:56.720
Mines that do not detonate either become further&nbsp;
buried or remain alongside the cleared path.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.440
Within military standards, this level of&nbsp;
effectiveness is considered acceptable, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:06.320
soldiers are trained to spot mines on the ground&nbsp;
and can more easily navigate and avoid them.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:11.320
The last 10 to 20% of mines are the&nbsp;
ones that stay in the ground. Or if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:16.320
a military operation does not pass through a&nbsp;
contaminated area the mines stay there until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:21.720
a civilian gets injured. Removing these&nbsp;
mines is known as Humanitarian demining.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.880
Ukraine is not the first country&nbsp;
to be scattered with mines in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:30.480
civilian areas. Cambodia was infested with&nbsp;
landmines during the Khmer Rouge regime.&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.800
Three decades later, only half of the land has&nbsp;
been cleared and they already have claimed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:40.280
lives of 64,000 people [17] [REF]. Even highly&nbsp;
trafficked tourist regions like Siem Riep have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:46.080
ominous mine warning signs, a constant reminder&nbsp;
not to venture off the beaten path surrounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:51.640
Cambodia's ancient temples, and the devastation&nbsp;
these mines have had on the civilian population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:56.960
is a confronting reminder to tourists of the&nbsp;
very recent genocide the country endured.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:03.080
In Afghanistan, landmines were indiscriminately&nbsp;
laid during the Soviet occupation between 1979 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:08.560
1992, and continue to be laid down by the Taliban&nbsp;
today. [18] [REF] This has injured or killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:16.080
almost 57, 000 people in the country. In 2021,&nbsp;
79% of the casualties were children. [19] [REF]

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:19.800
A small town in Mozambique was&nbsp;
evacuated due to rumors of mines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:24.120
in the surrounding area. Three months&nbsp;
later, only 4 were found [20] [REF].

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.040
The power of landmines comes&nbsp;
not only from their instant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:32.000
destructive capabilities but also due&nbsp;
to their ability to scare people away.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.160
These are inhumane weapons&nbsp;
that disproportionately kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:40.240
and maim innocent civilians long&nbsp;
after the conflict is resolved.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:43.680
In early 2022, the Kakhovka&nbsp;
dam near Kherson, Ukraine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:49.080
was destroyed. The estimated 500 mines in&nbsp;
the area that were not properly removed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:53.960
or destroyed by the military have now&nbsp;
potentially been dispersed by the floods.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:59.080
They may become buried deeper under debris or&nbsp;
end up in entirely new locations. [21] [REF]

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:02.400
Uncleared mines are a major&nbsp;
humanitarian challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:09.200
especially as the UN standard requires a&nbsp;
100% clearance rate within designated zones.

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:15.200
Ofcourse, to prevent harm from these mines,&nbsp;
the first step is to cease their use entirely.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:19.600
The Ottawa Treaty, also known as the&nbsp;
Mine Ban Treaty, prohibits the use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:22.400
production, stockpiling, and transfer of smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:26.280
anti-personnel mines, but it&nbsp;
excludes those targeting tanks.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:29.800
The Ottawa Treaty has been widely&nbsp;
embraced by the international community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:35.040
with over 160 signatory countries.&nbsp;
Unsurprisingly, it excludes Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:40.360
the US, China, India, and Pakistan,&nbsp;
the world's largest military forces.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:44.400
The U.S. reserved the right to use&nbsp;
so-called "non persistent landmines,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:49.640
which self-destruct or self-deactivate after 30&nbsp;
days to reduce the risk to civilians. [22] [REF]

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:54.440
The rationale behind this policy was to support&nbsp;
the defense of South Korea, where landmines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:59.320
played a significant role as a deterrent against&nbsp;
potential aggression from North Korea. [23] [REF]

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:05.160
Russia told the UN in 2020, that it “supports the&nbsp;
world without mines” but they still see them as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:10.360
essential tool to ensure border security. [REF].
 
When not on the front line, the method&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:14.800
for locating these mines primarily&nbsp;
involves the use of metal detectors.

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:17.268
Metal detectors are a simple tool.

00:11:17.268 --> 00:11:17.328
They are made out of a coil of&nbsp;
wire with a pulsing current,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.328 --> 00:11:18.000
which causes a changing magnetic field to&nbsp;
propagate through the ground. Any metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:23.200
in the soil interacts with this field,&nbsp;
and causes an audible beep for the user.

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:27.240
The user's sole source of information&nbsp;
is the strength of the beep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:32.320
which communicates the size and proximity&nbsp;
of the object. Consequently, operators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:36.720
must undergo rigorous training&nbsp;
to interpret the auditory cues.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.640
Upon hearing a beep, deminers&nbsp;
move to investigate the alarm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:44.680
The most common way to do this is&nbsp;
using a small stick. At an angle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:49.160
to avoid the top of the mines, they poke&nbsp;
the area in search of something solid.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:52.320
The probe can be anything from&nbsp;
a screwdriver to more sensitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:56.920
force-measuring probing sticks that help&nbsp;
assess the hardness of buried metal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:01.360
allowing for the differentiation of shrapnel&nbsp;
from potential landmines. [24] [REF]

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:07.200
This is a slow and tedious process that just&nbsp;
adds stress to the already pressured deminers.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:11.320
Here lies the problem with using these&nbsp;
simple tools, they detect any sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:15.920
metal in the ground, it can either be a&nbsp;
mine or some shrapnel. In war-torn areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:19.840
where shrapnel is common, soldiers&nbsp;
looking for mines get false alarms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:24.400
between 100 to 1000 times for every&nbsp;
mine they discover. [25] [REF]

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:29.920
To develop a more advanced demining system, the&nbsp;
U.S. Army initiated a substantial investment in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:36.160
1992, allocating a total of 73 million dollars&nbsp;
over a fifteen-year period [24] [REF]. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:40.360
objective was to establish the gold&nbsp;
standard in demining technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:45.320
resulting in the Handheld Standoff&nbsp;
Mine Detection System in 2006.

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:50.800
The system uses two sensors. Combining a metal&nbsp;
detector with a ground penetrating radar.

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:56.000
The radar gives the operator a better idea of the&nbsp;
size, geometry and depth of the detected object.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:01.000
Allowing them to filter out false positives&nbsp;
that metal detectors frequently provide.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:05.280
Since its introduction, it's been used&nbsp;
in Cambodia, Thailand, Afghanistan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:11.760
and Croatia to find over 56,000 mines and&nbsp;
reject 33 million false signals. [26] [REF]

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:16.920
Alternatively we can detect mines through their&nbsp;
smell. Dogs have been successfully trained to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:22.480
identify mines. Including the famous jack russell&nbsp;
terrier of the Ukraine war who has helped detect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:28.840
hundreds of explosives. Small dogs are ideal for&nbsp;
the job as they are too light to trigger mines.

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:31.040
Rats are useful for the same reason.

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:35.000
Off-the-shelf drones can detect&nbsp;
signs of mines that have been buried,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:39.200
and in situations where landmines are placed&nbsp;
directly on the ground or dispersed from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:43.960
air like the PFM-1, drones can simply go&nbsp;
up and spot them from above. [27][REF]

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:48.320
However, efforts are being made to develop&nbsp;
machine vision algorithms with both visible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:52.400
and infrared spectrums to detect these&nbsp;
landmines automatically, and they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:56.360
potentially be fitted with projectiles to&nbsp;
target the mine from the air. [28] [REF]

00:13:56.360 --> 00:14:00.560
Landmines will continue to plague&nbsp;
Ukraine for the coming decades even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:05.000
if the war ends now. Hopefully these&nbsp;
new techniques and technologies can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:08.520
accelerate the clearance of&nbsp;
this new colossal minefield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:14.080
but Ukraine also needs additional resources&nbsp;
to clear the way for their advancing soldiers.

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:20.120
So, to play our part we will be donating 2000&nbsp;
dollars to The Halo Trust an NGO dedicated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:25.200
clearing minefields around the world, including&nbsp;
Ukraine, while campaigning for the banning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:30.200
mines altogether. That money will be coming&nbsp;
directly out of our sponsor fee for Nebula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:36.880
and for every one of you that signs up to Nebula&nbsp;
we will donate another dollar to the cause.

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:40.000
It costs just 2.50 to sign&nbsp;
up to Nebula for a month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:44.480
so that’s 40% of your monthly fee going&nbsp;
directly to charity, and you will get&nbsp;&nbsp;

